WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:04.037
NARRATOR: For the past 20
years, New South Wales averaged

00:00:04.104 --> 00:00:06.139
four shark attacks a year.

00:00:06.206 --> 00:00:10.176
But in 2009, a
staggering 17 attacks

00:00:10.243 --> 00:00:15.281
occurred, with species
ranging from white sharks

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:16.683
to wobbegong sharks.

00:00:19.953 --> 00:00:24.324
With such a variety of
species behind the spike,

00:00:24.391 --> 00:00:27.594
investigators focus on
something that affects them all.

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:36.403
Marine biologist Holly Richmond
heads out to investigate.

00:00:40.407 --> 00:00:42.709
She dives in to
get a closer look.

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:49.783
Shark nets.

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:54.854
First deployed in 1937, they're
at more than 50 public beaches

00:00:54.921 --> 00:00:57.323
across New South Wales.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.260
Typically around
200 yards offshore,

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:02.996
these meshings form
an underwater fence

00:01:03.062 --> 00:01:05.131
with a simple purpose.

00:01:05.198 --> 00:01:07.700
Basically shark nets
they are culling device.

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:10.570
So their aim is to
catch and kill sharks.

00:01:10.637 --> 00:01:12.138
NARRATOR: Supporters
of the program

00:01:12.205 --> 00:01:13.940
believe that they've
helped keep sharks

00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:16.576
away from innocent beachgoers.

00:01:16.643 --> 00:01:20.713
But critics contend
there's a harmful downside.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.016
Putting a net in the
ocean, you're going to catch

00:01:23.082 --> 00:01:24.484
a variety of different animals.

00:01:24.551 --> 00:01:27.320
Rays, dolphins,
whales, sea turtles.

00:01:27.387 --> 00:01:28.688
There's been dugongs up North.

00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:30.390
There's a variety of
different animals.

00:01:30.457 --> 00:01:32.192
Pretty much anything
that's in the ocean

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:34.127
is going to get
tangled in these nets.

00:01:34.194 --> 00:01:36.496
And all this
bycatch could have

00:01:36.563 --> 00:01:40.133
an unintended side effect
tied to one of shark's

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.568
most important senses.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.039
Shark expert,
Doctor Vic Peddemors

00:01:46.105 --> 00:01:49.008
cuts in to get a look.

00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:52.178
The brain is really
the center point

00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:54.380
for all of the
different senses so

00:01:54.447 --> 00:01:57.517
that it can detect
food and other

00:01:57.584 --> 00:02:00.186
animals in the environment.

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:02.322
NARRATOR: If there's
bycatch stuck in a net,

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:05.525
a shark will use
two of its senses.

00:02:05.592 --> 00:02:09.128
Sharks have got a really,
really good sense of smell.

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:12.832
It allows them a
really acute ability

00:02:12.899 --> 00:02:20.173
to detect fish, blood, and
unusual smells in the water.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:21.541
NARRATOR: Additionally,
sharks use

00:02:21.608 --> 00:02:24.344
their lateral line
system to detect the prey

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.779
item moving in the water.

00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:30.884
So bycatch could in fact draw
sharks closer to the nets that

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.119
are intended to keep them away.

00:02:33.186 --> 00:02:36.489
And in 2009, reports
emerged that holes

00:02:36.556 --> 00:02:39.225
had formed in shark
nets similar to those

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:42.128
deployed in New South Wales.

00:02:42.195 --> 00:02:45.064
Could sharks, drawn
in by bycatch,

00:02:45.131 --> 00:02:47.834
be slipping through
flawed nets and coming

00:02:47.901 --> 00:02:50.470
into contact with humans?

